            <ArticleTitle>In Search of the 1-Hour Rule-Out for Acute Myocardial Infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1427-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1373/clinchem.2015.247536</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Old habits die hard. The study by Hillinger et al. provides a framework for several observations regarding the current state of ADPs for rapid rule-out of ACS. ADPs that incorporate serial assessments of high-sensitivity cardiac troponin appear to provide a viable approach to rule out ACS with sampling over 3 h with a high NPV. The actual interval (1, 2, or 3 h) and optimal thresholds are issues still to be refined for each assay. A dual-marker strategy using copeptin does not provide incremental clinical information beyond high-sensitivity cardiac troponin, and it seems unlikely that other biomarkers will substantially improve the NPV achieved with serial high-sensitivity cardiac troponin testing alone.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
